@page "/docs/get-started/from-radzen"

@* Moving an app from Radzen.Blazor: setup, then each piece with the Radzen code beside the
   IzzyBlazor code, the renamed members and what behaves differently. Written from a real port (an
   admin console on Radzen 12.1.2 that renders everything interactive with prerender off: buttons,
   nine data grids and DialogService, the rest hand-built) and checked against IzzyBlazor's source.
   Only what the port uses is mapped; the Radzen side is quoted from its code, not from Radzen's docs. *@

<ArticlePage Section="get-started" Slug="from-radzen">
    <Lead>
        Most of a Radzen app maps one to one: buttons, data grids and dialogs have IzzyBlazor counterparts, and much of what
        you built by hand around them (fields, tabs, menus, a side panel, a diff) is a component here. This page shows the
        Radzen code beside the IzzyBlazor code, what's renamed, and what behaves differently.
    </Lead>
    <ChildContent>
        <Heading Level="2">At a glance</Heading>
        <Table Items="Glance" Caption="Radzen and IzzyBlazor" Size="ControlSize.Sm" Class="site-params" Bordered="false">
            <TemplateColumn Title="Radzen, or built by hand" Context="row">@Rich(row.Radzen)</TemplateColumn>
            <TemplateColumn Title="IzzyBlazor" Context="row">@Rich(row.Izzy)</TemplateColumn>
        </Table>
        <TextBlock>
            The two libraries can run side by side while you move page by page, as in
            <Anchor Href="docs/get-started/existing-app">Add to an existing app</Anchor>. Some types have the same name in
            both: <Code>DialogService</Code>, <Code>DialogOptions</Code>, <Code>ConfirmOptions</Code>, <Code>Dialog</Code>,
            <Code>Tooltip</Code>, <Code>TextAlign</Code>, <Code>Orientation</Code>, <Code>IconStyle</Code>,
            <Code>SidebarPosition</Code> and <Code>ColorScheme</Code>. A file with <Code>@@using</Code> for both gets
            ambiguous references, so move a file to IzzyBlazor whole, or qualify the name
            (<Code>IzzyBlazor.DialogService</Code>). Components of your own can clash too, such as a <Code>Menu</Code>:
            rename yours, or replace it with IzzyBlazor's.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Setup</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            Replace Radzen's services, stylesheet, script and <Code>&lt;RadzenComponents /&gt;</Code>. IzzyBlazor's theme is
            <Code>&lt;IzzyTheme /&gt;</Code> in the head, and there's no script tag: Blazor loads IzzyBlazor's script
            itself. <Code>DialogHost</Code> is where <Code>DialogService</Code> shows its dialogs, and it must be
            interactive. When <Code>Routes</Code> is interactive, put it in the layout as it is; otherwise give it a render
            mode: <Code>&lt;DialogHost @@rendermode="InteractiveServer" /&gt;</Code>.
        </TextBlock>
        @SideBySide(SetupRadzen, SetupIzzy)
        <TextBlock>
            Replace <Code>@@using Radzen</Code> and <Code>@@using Radzen.Blazor</Code> in <Code>_Imports.razor</Code> with
            <Code>@@using IzzyBlazor</Code>, and remove the stylesheets you wrote to adjust Radzen's <Code>rz-</Code> classes.
            The rest of setup, such as your brand color in <Code>IzzyTheme</Code>, is in
            <Anchor Href="docs/get-started/blazor-web-app">Blazor Web App</Anchor> and
            <Anchor Href="docs/theming/themes">Themes and presets</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Buttons</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            <Code>RadzenButton</Code> becomes <Code>Button</Code>, with its text as content. A button with only an icon is an
            <Code>IconButton</Code>, which needs a <Code>Label</Code> for screen readers; icons are Lucide's
            (<Code>Lucide.X</Code>), not Material names. Radzen's <Code>rz-button</Code> classes on a link become a
            <Code>Button</Code> with <Code>Href</Code>, and on a submit button a <Code>Button</Code> with
            <Code>Submit</Code>.
        </TextBlock>
        @SideBySide(ButtonsRadzen, ButtonsIzzy)
        <Table Items="ButtonRenames" Caption="RadzenButton members" Size="ControlSize.Sm" Class="site-params" Bordered="false">
            <TemplateColumn Title="RadzenButton" Context="row">@Rich(row.Radzen)</TemplateColumn>
            <TemplateColumn Title="Button" Context="row">@Rich(row.Izzy)</TemplateColumn>
        </Table>
        <TextBlock>
            <Code>id</Code>, <Code>aria-label</Code> and other attributes go on the <Code>button</Code> element, as before, so
            selectors like <Code>#key-create</Code> keep working. <Code>Loading</Code> keeps the button focusable and shows a
            spinner, where <Code>Disabled</Code> alone gives no sign that something is happening. See
            <Anchor Href="docs/components/button">Button</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Data grids</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            <Code>RadzenDataGrid</Code> becomes <Code>Table</Code>. A column bound to a property is a
            <Code>PropertyColumn</Code>, taking an expression instead of the property's name; a column with a template is a
            <Code>TemplateColumn</Code>, whose content is the template. There's no <Code>Columns</Code> wrapper, and
            <Code>TItem</Code> comes from <Code>Items</Code>.
        </TextBlock>
        @SideBySide(GridRadzen, GridIzzy)
        <Table Items="GridRenames" Caption="RadzenDataGrid members" Size="ControlSize.Sm" Class="site-params" Bordered="false">
            <TemplateColumn Title="RadzenDataGrid" Context="row">@Rich(row.Radzen)</TemplateColumn>
            <TemplateColumn Title="Table" Context="row">@Rich(row.Izzy)</TemplateColumn>
        </Table>
        <TextBlock>What behaves differently:</TextBlock>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>
                <strong>Sorting, paging and filters go in the address.</strong> A sortable header is a link that sets
                <Code>?sort=email</Code>, page numbers set <Code>?page=2</Code>, and a filter sets <Code>?f-email=</Code>.
                The view survives a reload and can be shared, and the back button undoes a sort. Two tables on one page share
                these keys unless one has a <Code>QueryPrefix</Code>. To keep the state in the component instead, as Radzen
                does, bind it: <Code>@@bind-Sort</Code>, <Code>@@bind-Page</Code>, <Code>@@bind-Filter</Code>.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                <strong>Filters are in the header, not in a row under it.</strong> A filterable column has a filter button that
                opens its field, and the filters in use show as removable tags above the table. Text matches anywhere and always
                ignores case. There's no filter row and no choice of operator.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                <strong>Columns opt in.</strong> Radzen sorts and filters every column unless told not to; a
                <Code>Table</Code> column sorts or filters only with <Code>Sortable</Code> or <Code>Filterable</Code>.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                <strong>A template column sorts by what you say.</strong> Radzen sorts a template column by its
                <Code>Property</Code>; a <Code>TemplateColumn</Code> needs <Code>SortBy</Code> and a <Code>SortKey</Code> for
                the address, and <Code>FilterBy</Code> to filter.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                <strong><Code>Caption</Code> names the table</strong> for screen readers; it isn't shown. An <Code>id</Code> goes
                on the table's outer element, so <Code>#members tr</Code> still finds the rows.
            </ListItem>
        </List>
        <TextBlock>
            See <Anchor Href="docs/components/table">Table</Anchor> for an <Code>ItemsProvider</Code> that loads each page
            from a server; its <Code>DataRequest</Code> carries the sort, the range and the filter, and
            <Code>ToResultAsync</Code> applies them to an EF Core query, as <Code>LoadDataArgs</Code> with Dynamic LINQ did.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Paging by cursor</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            A list that pages by an opaque "next" token, with no total, can be a <Code>Table</Code> too. Set
            <Code>Paging</Code> and return the next cursor from the <Code>ItemsProvider</Code>; the table passes it back as
            the request's <Code>Cursor</Code>. <Code>TablePaging.LoadMore</Code> puts a button under the rows that adds the
            next page to them, as a hand-built "Load earlier" button does.
        </TextBlock>
        @SideBySide(CursorBefore, CursorIzzy, "Built by hand")
        <TextBlock>
            The table has an error state: when the provider throws, it shows "Couldn't load the rows." with Retry, and with
            Load more the rows already shown stay and the message goes under them. So the provider throws on a failed
            request instead of keeping a message of its own. <Code>ReloadAsync()</Code> loads the list again from where it began, for a new search. With an
            <Code>ItemKey</Code>, a row that's already shown isn't added twice. <Code>TablePaging.NextPrevious</Code> shows
            First, Previous and Next instead, and <Code>LoadOnScroll</Code> loads as the last row comes into view; see
            <Anchor Href="docs/components/table#paging-without-a-total">Paging without a total</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Dialogs</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            <Code>DialogService</Code> keeps its name and its <Code>OpenAsync&lt;T&gt;(title, parameters, options)</Code>.
            It returns a <Code>DialogResult</Code> instead of <Code>dynamic</Code>: <Code>Data</Code> is what the dialog
            closed with, and <Code>Canceled</Code> is true when it was dismissed. The width is a <Code>Length</Code>, or one
            of the named sizes: <Code>Sm</Code> is 384px, <Code>Md</Code> (the default) 512px, <Code>Lg</Code> 768px and
            <Code>Xl</Code> 1024px. On a phone the dialog still fits the screen.
        </TextBlock>
        @SideBySide(OpenRadzen, OpenIzzy, language: "csharp")
        <TextBlock>
            Inside the dialog, close it through the <Code>DialogInstance</Code> it cascades, not the service.
            <Code>Close(data)</Code> ends <Code>OpenAsync</Code> with that data, and <Code>Cancel()</Code> ends it as
            canceled. A Cancel button that reports something, such as how many people were added before one was refused,
            calls <Code>Close</Code> with that instead.
        </TextBlock>
        @SideBySide(InsideRadzen, InsideIzzy)
        <TextBlock>
            <Code>DialogService.Close(data)</Code> is still there and closes the most recent dialog, as Radzen's does, but
            <Code>DialogInstance</Code> closes only its own. Closing a dialog that has already ended does nothing, so a
            request that comes back after its dialog was closed can't close the next one.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            <Code>Confirm</Code> becomes <Code>ConfirmAsync</Code>, with the same message, title and options, and returns a
            <Code>bool</Code> rather than a <Code>bool?</Code>. Give a removal <Code>Accent.Danger</Code> to make the confirm
            button red:
        </TextBlock>
        @SideBySide(ConfirmRadzen, ConfirmIzzy, language: "csharp")
        <Table Items="DialogRenames" Caption="DialogService members" Size="ControlSize.Sm" Class="site-params" Bordered="false">
            <TemplateColumn Title="Radzen" Context="row">@Rich(row.Radzen)</TemplateColumn>
            <TemplateColumn Title="IzzyBlazor" Context="row">@Rich(row.Izzy)</TemplateColumn>
        </Table>
        <TextBlock>What behaves differently:</TextBlock>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>
                <strong>Focus moves in as the dialog opens.</strong> The dialog is the browser's own (<Code>showModal</Code>),
                so focus goes to the field with <Code>autofocus</Code>, else the first thing that takes focus, with no timer.
                Radzen moves it half a second later, so a test helper that waits for that, or for a timer left by the last
                dialog, can go.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                <strong>Every dismissal is a cancel.</strong> Escape, a click on the backdrop and the close button end
                <Code>OpenAsync</Code> with <Code>Canceled</Code> true and no data.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                <strong>A confirm starts on Cancel.</strong> Cancel has the focus and Escape cancels. A confirm has no close
                button and ignores clicks on the backdrop, so it's answered with one of its buttons or Escape.
            </ListItem>
        </List>
        <TextBlock>
            See <Anchor Href="docs/components/dialog">Dialog</Anchor>, which also covers dialogs written in the page.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Tests</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            In bUnit, register IzzyBlazor's services instead of Radzen's. <Code>DialogService</Code> is sealed, so instead of
            a subclass that records what it was closed with, render a <Code>DialogHost</Code>, open the dialog through the
            service and await what it returns. The host shows dialogs only when it renders interactively, so set the
            renderer to interactive with <Code>SetRendererInfo</Code>. A <Code>TextField</Code> takes its value on change,
            so the test calls <Code>Change</Code> where it called <Code>Input</Code>.
        </TextBlock>
        @SideBySide(BunitRadzen, BunitIzzy, language: "csharp")
        <TextBlock>
            Open the dialog in a block lambda, as here: an expression lambda returns the task, and bUnit's
            <Code>InvokeAsync</Code> then waits for the dialog to close before going on. A test that presses a button
            through the component finds <Code>Button</Code> and calls its <Code>OnClick</Code>, where it found
            <Code>RadzenButton</Code> and its <Code>Click</Code>; the attributes are in <Code>AdditionalAttributes</Code>.
            Only <Code>ToastService</Code> calls JavaScript, so other components need no JS interop set up.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>Browser tests that find Radzen's classes change to these:</TextBlock>
        <Table Items="Selectors" Caption="Test selectors" Size="ControlSize.Sm" Class="site-params" Bordered="false">
            <TemplateColumn Title="Radzen" Context="row">@Rich(row.Radzen)</TemplateColumn>
            <TemplateColumn Title="IzzyBlazor" Context="row">@Rich(row.Izzy)</TemplateColumn>
        </Table>
        <TextBlock>
            A dialog is named by its title, so finding it by role (<Code>GetByRole(AriaRole.Dialog, new() { Name = "Remove from
            tenant?" })</Code>) holds up better than a class. A confirm without a title is named by its message. A test that
            checks the page's stylesheets looks for <Code>_content/IzzyBlazor.Core/izzyblazor.css</Code>, with
            <Code>@@Assets</Code>'s fingerprint, and no script tag.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Fields</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            Hand-built fields (a <Code>label</Code>, an <Code>input</Code> or <Code>textarea</Code> with Radzen's classes, a
            hint) become <Code>TextField</Code>, <Code>TextArea</Code>, <Code>Select</Code> or <Code>DateField</Code>, which
            draw the label, hint and errors and link them for screen readers. An <Code>id</Code> goes on the input.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            A <Code>DateField</Code> reads and shows the time in UTC unless you set <Code>TimeZone</Code>. Bind a
            <Code>DateTimeOffset</Code> for a date and time, or a <Code>DateOnly</Code> for a date alone. A
            <Code>DateTime</Code> works too: one that isn't <Code>Local</Code> is read as UTC, and on an interactive page it
            comes back with <Code>DateTimeKind.Utc</Code>.
        </TextBlock>
        @SideBySide(FieldsBefore, FieldsIzzy, "Built by hand")
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>
                <strong>The value changes when the field does</strong>, on leaving it or pressing Enter, as with Blazor's
                <Code>InputText</Code>. A field bound with <Code>@@bind:event="oninput"</Code>, so that a Save button turns on
                as you type, needs an <Code>@@oninput</Code> handler on the field as well, which goes to the input.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                <strong>Errors come from the form.</strong> A field shows its <Code>EditContext</Code>'s messages for its
                property. In a <Code>Form</Code>, add an API's refusal to a <Code>ValidationMessageStore</Code> and the field
                shows it; outside one, keep showing the message yourself.
            </ListItem>
        </List>
        <TextBlock>
            See <Anchor Href="docs/components/text-field">TextField</Anchor>,
            <Anchor Href="docs/components/date-field">DateField</Anchor> and
            <Anchor Href="docs/common/forms">Forms and validation</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">A diff</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            A hand-drawn line diff (old and new line numbers, added and removed marks, folded runs of unchanged lines) is a
            <Code>Diff</Code>. Give it the two texts and it compares them, colors them as <Code>Code</Code> does and marks the
            changed words. The notes for identical texts and for texts too far apart to compare are built in, with
            <Code>SameText</Code> and <Code>TooManyText</Code> to change them; <Code>MaxChanges</Code> is 1,000 lines unless
            set.
        </TextBlock>
        @SideBySide(DiffBefore, DiffIzzy, "Built by hand")
        <TextBlock>
            For a diff computed elsewhere, pass <Code>Lines</Code> instead; <Code>TextDiff.Compare</Code> is the comparison
            on its own. <Code>Layout="DiffLayout.Split"</Code> puts the old text beside the new. See
            <Anchor Href="docs/components/diff">Diff</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">A side panel</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            For a list with a panel beside it about the chosen row, put an <Code>AppAside</Code> in the page. It takes the
            shell's aside column for that page alone, scrolls on its own, and below its breakpoint goes under the main area
            with <Code>SmallScreen="AsideSmallScreen.Below"</Code>. The rows link to the chosen one with
            <Code>RowHref</Code>, and <Code>RowCurrent</Code> marks it. The panel's tabs are a <Code>Tabs</Code> of
            <Code>TabLink</Code>s (more on those below).
        </TextBlock>
        @SideBySide(PanelBefore, PanelIzzy, "Built by hand")
        <TextBlock>
            This needs the layout to be an <Anchor Href="docs/components/app-shell">AppShell</Anchor>, with your bar as its
            <Code>AppHeader</Code> and <Code>@@Body</Code> in its <Code>AppMain</Code>. The page's aside moves into the shell
            when the page and the layout render in the same mode, as they do when <Code>Routes</Code> is interactive. Use
            <Code>AsideSmallScreen.Drawer</Code> instead for a panel people open from an <Code>AsideToggle</Code> on small
            screens.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            A tab link is current when the address matches it, query included. When the page shows a default the address
            doesn't name, such as the first row before one is chosen, say which tab is current with <Code>Current</Code>, as
            the panel above does.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Tabs and menus</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            A <Code>nav</Code> of links marked with <Code>aria-current</Code> is a <Code>Tabs</Code> of <Code>TabLink</Code>s.
            The server marks the tab for the current address, and a page below a tab's address keeps that tab marked. When
            the tabs are one page told apart by the query (<Code>?tab=record</Code>), only the tab whose query matches is
            marked. Tabs whose panels are on the same page are <Code>Tab</Code>s with content.
        </TextBlock>
        @SideBySide(TabsBefore, TabsIzzy, "Built by hand")
        <TextBlock>
            A menu component of your own, which closes on Escape, on a click outside and on navigation, and moves focus into
            its panel, becomes IzzyBlazor's <Code>Menu</Code>. Opening, Escape and a click outside are the browser's (the
            Popover API). Opening focuses the first item, arrow keys move between items, typing jumps to one, and Escape puts
            focus back on the button. A <Code>MenuRadioGroup</Code> has a heading and marks the chosen item from its
            <Code>Value</Code>; its items can still be links.
        </TextBlock>
        @SideBySide(MenuBefore, MenuIzzy, "Your own component")
        <TextBlock>
            <Code>Label</Code> is the button's text, and its name for screen readers. A name that doesn't contain the visible
            text, such as an <Code>aria-label</Code> of "Switch tenant or environment" on a button that reads "Alder ·
            Production", fails WCAG's label-in-name rule, so the visible text names it. See
            <Anchor Href="docs/components/tabs">Tabs</Anchor> and <Anchor Href="docs/components/menu">Menu</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Next steps</Heading>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/components">Components</Anchor>: Stepper, Switch, SegmentedControl and the rest of what you may have built by hand.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/common/render-modes">Render modes</Anchor>: what changes if some pages become static.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/theming/tokens">Tokens and customizing</Anchor>: CSS variables for your own styles beside the components.</ListItem>
        </List>
    </ChildContent>
</ArticlePage>

@code {
    // Radzen's code (or the hand-built markup) on the left, IzzyBlazor's on the right; one under
    // the other until the content is wide enough for both.
    private static RenderFragment SideBySide(string before, string after, string beforeTitle = "Radzen", string language = "razor") =>
        @<Grid MinChildWidth="Length.Rem(26)" Gap="3">
            <Code Block Language="@language" Title="@beforeTitle">@before</Code>
            <Code Block Language="@language" Title="IzzyBlazor">@after</Code>
        </Grid>;

    private sealed record Mapping(string Radzen, string Izzy);

    private static Mapping Map(string radzen, string izzy) => new(radzen, izzy);

    // A table cell: text, with the parts between backticks as code.
    private static RenderFragment Rich(string text) => builder =>
    {
        var parts = text.Split('`');
        for (var i = 0; i < parts.Length; i++)
        {
            if (i % 2 == 1)
            {
                builder.OpenElement(0, "code");
                builder.AddContent(1, parts[i]);
                builder.CloseElement();
            }
            else
            {
                builder.AddContent(2, parts[i]);
            }
        }
    };

    private static readonly Mapping[] Glance =
    [
        Map("`RadzenButton`", "`Button`, `IconButton`"),
        Map("`RadzenDataGrid`", "`Table`, with `PropertyColumn` and `TemplateColumn`"),
        Map("`DialogService`", "`DialogService`, with `DialogHost` in the layout"),
        Map("`<RadzenComponents />`", "`<DialogHost />`, and `<ToastRegion />` for toasts"),
        Map("`<input>`, `<textarea>`, `<select>`", "`TextField`, `TextArea`, `Select`, `Combobox`, `NumberField`"),
        Map("`<input type=\"date\">`, `\"datetime-local\"`", "`DateField`"),
        Map("A list paged by cursor", "`Table` with `Paging`"),
        Map("A line diff", "`Diff`"),
        Map("`<main>` and `<aside>`", "`AppShell`, with an `AppAside` in the page"),
        Map("A `nav` of links as tabs", "`Tabs` with `TabLink`"),
        Map("A dropdown menu", "`Menu`"),
    ];

    private static readonly Mapping[] ButtonRenames =
    [
        Map("`Text=\"Save\"`", "Content: `<Button>Save</Button>`"),
        Map("`Click`", "`OnClick`"),
        Map("`ButtonStyle.Primary`", "`Accent.Primary` (the default)"),
        Map("`ButtonStyle.Base`", "`Accent.Neutral`"),
        Map("`ButtonStyle.Danger`", "`Accent.Danger`"),
        Map("`Variant.Filled`", "`ButtonVariant.Solid` (the default)"),
        Map("`Variant.Outlined`", "`ButtonVariant.Outline`"),
        Map("`Variant.Text`", "`ButtonVariant.Ghost`"),
        Map("`ButtonSize.Small`", "`ControlSize.Sm`"),
        Map("`Icon=\"close\"` with no text", "`IconButton` with `Icon=\"Lucide.X\"` and a `Label`"),
        Map("`IsBusy`", "`Loading`"),
        Map("`class=\"rz-button …\"` on an `<a>`", "`Button` with `Href`"),
        Map("`class=\"rz-button …\"` on a submit `<button>`", "`Button` with `Submit`"),
    ];

    private static readonly Mapping[] GridRenames =
    [
        Map("`Data`", "`Items`"),
        Map("`TItem`", "Inferred from `Items`"),
        Map("`<Columns>`", "None: columns go straight inside"),
        Map("`RadzenDataGridColumn Property=\"Email\"`", "`PropertyColumn Property=\"m => m.Email\"`"),
        Map("A column with a `<Template>`", "`TemplateColumn`, with the template as its content"),
        Map("`AllowSorting` on the grid", "`Sortable` on each column"),
        Map("`AllowFiltering`, `FilterMode.Simple`", "`Filterable` on each column"),
        Map("`FilterCaseSensitivity.CaseInsensitive`", "None: text filters always ignore case"),
        Map("`Sortable=\"false\"`, `Filterable=\"false\"`", "Leave `Sortable` or `Filterable` off"),
        Map("`AllowPaging`, `PageSize=\"50\"`", "`PageSize=\"50\"`"),
        Map("`Width=\"200px\"`", "`Width=\"Length.Px(200)\"`"),
        Map("`TextAlign=\"TextAlign.End\"`", "`Align=\"CellAlign.End\"`"),
    ];

    private static readonly Mapping[] DialogRenames =
    [
        Map("`OpenAsync<T>(…)` returns `dynamic`", "`OpenAsync<T>(…)` returns a `DialogResult`: `Data` and `Canceled`"),
        Map("`DialogOptions.Width = \"560px\"`", "`Width = Length.Px(560)`, or `Size = DialogSize.Md`"),
        Map("`Confirm(message, title, options)`", "`ConfirmAsync(message, title, options)`"),
        Map("`Confirm` returns `bool?`", "Returns `bool`"),
        Map("`ConfirmOptions.OkButtonText`", "`ConfirmText`"),
        Map("`ConfirmOptions.CancelButtonText`", "`CancelText`"),
        Map("`Dialogs.Close(result)` in the dialog", "`Dialog.Close(result)`, on the cascaded `DialogInstance`"),
        Map("`Dialogs.Close(null)` in the dialog", "`Dialog.Cancel()`"),
    ];

    private static readonly Mapping[] Selectors =
    [
        Map("`.rz-dialog-confirm`", "The dialog by role and name, or `dialog:has(.ib-confirm-message)`"),
        Map("`.rz-dialog-title`", "`.ib-dialog-title` (an `h2`)"),
        Map("`.rz-dialog-confirm-message`", "`.ib-confirm-message`"),
        Map("`.rz-dialog`", "`dialog`, or `.ib-dialog`"),
    ];

    private const string SetupRadzen = """
        // Program.cs
        builder.Services.AddRadzenComponents();

        <!-- App.razor -->
        <link rel="stylesheet" href=
          "_content/Radzen.Blazor/css/standard-base.css"
        />
        ...
        <script src="_framework/blazor.web.js"></script>
        <script src=
          "_content/Radzen.Blazor/Radzen.Blazor.js">
        </script>

        <!-- MainLayout.razor -->
        <RadzenComponents />
        """;

    private const string SetupIzzy = """
        // Program.cs
        builder.Services.AddIzzyBlazor();
        ...
        app.MapIzzyBlazor();

        <!-- App.razor -->
        <link rel="stylesheet" href=
